{"stage": "step:s2", "served_model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4096, "output_tokens": 449, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "I ran the resolution simulation for all three technologies at 50, 100, 150, 200 and 300 \u00b5m (unirradiated) and recorded all 15 results.\n\n| Thickness (\u00b5m) | Sensor-T1 (\u00b5m) | Sensor-T2 (\u00b5m) | Sensor-T3 (\u00b5m) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 50 | 10.56 | 6.56 | 16.01 |\n| 100 | 9.19 | 6.00 | 14.19 |\n| 150 | 9.17 | 6.28 | 14.27 |\n| 200 | 9.30 | 6.05 | 14.76 |\n| 300 | 12.42 | 8.12 | 18.49 |\n\nEach technology has a resolution minimum at roughly 100\u2013200 \u00b5m, and resolution degrades at 300 \u00b5m. Sensor-T2 is the best on resolution at every thickness, but its hit efficiency at 2e16 neq/cm\u00b2 was previously recorded as 82%, below the 97% hard requirement. That leaves Sensor-T1 and Sensor-T3 as the candidates that meet the efficiency constraint. Between them, Sensor-T1 (about 9.2 \u00b5m at 100\u2013200 \u00b5m) resolves better than Sensor-T3 (about 14 \u00b5m). This step only maps resolution, so the final recommendation is for a later step to make.", "type": "text"}]}
