{"stage": "step:s3", "served_model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 3533, "output_tokens": 418, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "content": [{"text": "**Step s3 result: the efficiency filter removes Sensor-T2 and keeps Sensor-T1 and Sensor-T3.**\n\n| Technology | Hit efficiency at 2e16 neq/cm\u00b2 | vs. 97% requirement | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sensor-T1 | 98.5% | +1.5 pts | Survives |\n| Sensor-T2 | 82.0% | \u221215 pts | **Eliminated** (strong charge trapping; full depletion not reachable at max bias) |\n| Sensor-T3 | 99.2% | +2.2 pts | Survives |\n\n**Caveat on thickness:** the irradiation test-beam document gives one efficiency value per technology and does not state the sensor thickness it was measured on. So the data cannot show which thicknesses keep efficiency at or above 97%. The survivors are therefore established at the technology level only. Any thickness choice for T1 or T3 is unverified for efficiency until irradiated data exist at that thickness. This matters most at the thick end (for example 300 \u00b5m), where charge trapping after 2e16 is more severe, although the documents give no numbers for that.\n\nThe recorded results mark T1 and T3 as passing and T2 as failing, with the margins above.", "type": "text"}]}
